Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Sentinel Foundry**, a tall assembly tower whose
stamping press hammers down and back up while its drive gear spins — as a 56x76x56
opaque-voxel model using only the `voxel-anim` tool. You build it by **painting
discrete opaque cells** — one recorded operation at a time — and you **author its
stamp and spin animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Foundry is* and *how it must move*, but the parts you build, how
you break the tower into moving pieces, where you place the joints, and how you shape
every form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces a hammering, spinning
foundry needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. It is the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation
at a time**, and the ordered operations you issue — recorded per part to
`parts/<part>.actions.json` — together with the rig you define are the **authoritative
output that is scored**, not the voxels on disk. Anything produced another way (writing
files directly, editing a preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the recorded
operations and the rig count.

Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`; each part is sculpted
**separately** in its own preview and log. The volume starts **empty**. Voxels are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` colors — there is no transparency. After each call the binary
re-renders that part's isometric preview to `parts/<part>.png` and appends the
operation to that part's log.

**You invent the rig.** Define whatever parts you need with `voxel-anim define-part`
(giving each a parent and a pivot), place the joints they move on with
`voxel-anim define-joint`, and author the required animations with `define-animation`/
`add-keyframe`. Every voxel operation targets a **part** with `--part <name>`, so
define a part before you paint into it; each part is a **separate** volume in the
shared coordinates. The field starts **empty**.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its part, for example:

```
voxel-anim define-part --name tower
voxel-anim fill-box --part tower --x0 6 --y0 0 --z0 6 --x1 49 --y1 60 --z1 49 --color "#c69a4b"
```

(The part names above are just an example — name your own parts however you like.) Run
`voxel-anim --help` to list every operation **and** the rig and animation subcommands
(defining parts, setting pivots, defining joints, and authoring animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `voxel-anim <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`parts/<part>.png` after your calls** to see that part's progress, **and read the
assembled-scene previews under `scene/` (`scene/iso.png`, `scene/front.png`,
`scene/side.png`, `scene/top.png`)** — the whole foundry composed from all your parts,
re-rendered after every operation. The scene views are how you confirm the parts
actually fit together: that the press rides centered in the tower's throat and the gear
seats on its flank. Because each part is sculpted in the shared volume's coordinates,
position each part where it belongs on the finished tower; the scene simply composes
them there.

